think The prune log named the crux-first next sibling: a BRANCH / JUNCTION detector (degree three or more), the other half of skeleton topology beside endpoints. Studied endpoints.rye, hitmiss.rye, structure.rye to compose correctly.
think The crux, reasoned before coding: an endpoint is degree one (eight configs, so endpoints used a hit-or-miss template pack). A junction is degree three or more -- C(8,3)+...+C(8,8) = 219 configs, too many for a pack. A naive neighbor COUNT is wrong too: it false-flags the pixels flanking an orthogonal junction (the pixel below a plus's center is 8-adjacent to the center's two arm pixels, so a count reads degree four there). The correct tool is the CROSSING NUMBER (Rutovitz connectivity): walk the eight neighbors in a ring, count the foreground ARCS as rising edges. Endpoint one arc, path two, junction three or more; a lone dot and a solid interior zero. Hand-verified the plus flanker reads crossing number TWO (one arc) while the center reads four -- the decisive separation.
think Built image/branch.rye: detect_branches lights a foreground pixel iff its crossing number >= branch_min_crossing (3). Owns only the crossing-number gather (the eight-neighbor ring N,NE,E,SE,S,SW,W,NW and its arc count) and the threshold; reads the binary meaning (red >= 128) from hitmiss.hitmiss_on_threshold (one source of truth shared with endpoints); qoi owns the buffers. An out-of-bounds neighbor is GROUND never a phantom -- the honest topology for a degree measure, diverging on purpose from the rank-filter clamp structure uses (where a clamped border is right for a max or min). No division: rising-edge count equals the crossing number directly.
think Wrote tools/branch_witness.rish per the REDS #85 lesson -- native `assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-branch"`, never a shell grep over interpolated output; the GREEN substring is apostrophe-free.
obs GREEN on the FIRST full metal run, no reds -- the ring geometry traced by hand before coding paid off, exactly the family discipline. The cruxes held: DIAGONAL-Y (the proven prune geometry: one meeting point of crossing number three, endpoints and path dark), ORTHOGONAL-PLUS (only the degree-four center lights, the four crossing-number-two flankers dark -- the decisive proof we count arcs not neighbors), DEGENERATE-ZERO (a lone dot and a solid interior both light nothing), opacity from a translucent source, QOI round-trip byte-for-byte, empty-source refusal by name, determinism.
obs The crossing number is genuinely the hardest-solvable-that's-right move: a naive degree count would have shipped a junction detector that fires five times on a plain plus. Choosing the crossing number is why the plus test reads exactly one junction. Crux-first served the correctness, not just the count.
obs tame_style_check bans clean (no compound asserts, no banned seams), width-check exit 0, plain 7-bit ASCII on both new files, living_docs_lint advisory-GREEN, living_card_ascii_witness GREEN after the REMEMBER repoint. REMEMBER kept single-stranded: the live-edge and Git-nib repointed to branch, prune folded to a one-line prior-nib pointer, and the giant orphaned older-trail paragraph condensed into four compact prior-nib comments (no fact lost -- all in session-logs and git history; no cairn, a one-line repoint). image/README.md seats the Branch / junction entry after Prune.
obs ORGANIZING.md (council rota item 21) deep-read -- it confirmed placement: module code in its home, the witness in tools/, the reasoning in session-logs/, next in crux/. Noted in passing that ORGANIZING still names work-in-progress/ as the workbench where the tree renamed it crux/ (REDS #84's rename); a docs-sync ratchet for a later touch, not this lap's narrow scope.
